## Task
Three Critical security/functional bugs: anonymous `/register` accepts client-chosen `Role`; `ForgotPassword`/`ResetPassword` never commit AND the `PasswordResetTokens` table doesn't exist; `RevokedReasonIsReuse` is computed in memory but never persisted (column missing).

## Details
**(a) Self-Admin via `/register`:** `AuthController.cs:28-46`:
```csharp
[HttpPost("register"), AllowAnonymous]
public async Task<IActionResult> Register(RegisterRequest req, ...) {
    RoleName? role = null;
    if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(req.Role) && Enum.TryParse<RoleName>(req.Role, true, out var r))
        role = r;
    var result = await register.ExecuteAsync(new RegisterCommand(req.Email, req.Password, ..., role, ...), ...);
}
// RegisterHandler.cs:29
var role = cmd.Role ?? RoleName.Regular;
var user = User.Create(..., role, cmd.MemberId);
```
Any unauthenticated caller can POST `{"role":"Admin"}` and become Admin.

**(b) ForgotPassword/ResetPassword never commit + missing table:** `ForgotPasswordHandler.cs:11-29`, `ResetPasswordHandler.cs:13-39`:
```csharp
public sealed class ForgotPasswordHandler(
    IUserRepository userRepo,
    IPasswordResetTokenRepository tokenRepo)        // no IUnitOfWork
{
    public async Task<Result> ExecuteAsync(...) {
        ...
        await tokenRepo.AddAsync(resetToken, ct);
        return Result.Success();   // never committed
    }
}
```
`infra/init.sql:1-44` declares only `Users` and `RefreshTokens` — no `PasswordResetTokens` table. Repository queries throw `PostgresException: relation "passwordresettokens" does not exist`. Endpoint hard-crashes (caught by `ExceptionHandlingMiddleware` → 500). "User enumeration prevention" (audit Good #7) returns 200 on unknown email, masking that the feature is dead.

**(c) `RevokedReasonIsReuse` computed but not persisted:** `RefreshToken.cs:17,65-70` sets the flag in the aggregate; `RefreshTokenRepository.cs:42-57` UPDATE omits `RevokedReasonIsReuse`; `infra/init.sql` doesn't declare the column. The audit praiseworthy "family-revocation pattern" cannot record *why* a revocation happened — the security-anomaly audit trail is fictional.

## Affected Files
- `src/TSQR.Autheo.WebApi/Controllers/AuthController.cs:28-46` (drop `Role` from public `RegisterRequest`)
- `src/TSQR.Autheo.Application/Commands/RegisterHandler.cs:29` (force `RoleName.Regular` for public registrations)
- `src/TSQR.Autheo.Application/Commands/ForgotPasswordHandler.cs` / `ResetPasswordHandler.cs` (inject `IUnitOfWork`; `await uow.SaveChangesAsync(ct)` at the end)
- `infra/init.sql` (add `PasswordResetTokens` table with `TokenHash VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL UNIQUE` + `RevokedReasonIsReuse BOOLEAN NULL` column on `RefreshTokens`)
- `src/TSQR.Autheo.Infrastructure/Dapper/Repositories/RefreshTokenRepository.cs:42-57` (include `RevokedReasonIsReuse` in SELECT / INSERT / UPDATE)
- `RefreshToken.Rehydrate` (hydrate the column on read)

## Suggested Approach
1. Strip `Role` from `RegisterRequest`; force `RoleName.Regular` in `RegisterHandler`. Role elevation is only via a future admin-only endpoint.
2. Inject `IUnitOfWork` into both password-reset handlers; commit at the end.
3. Add `PasswordResetTokens` table to `init.sql`; match the schema in `PasswordResetTokenRepository`.
4. Add `RevokedReasonIsReuse BOOLEAN NULL` column to `RefreshTokens`; include in SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE; hydrate in `RefreshToken.Rehydrate`.
5. Wire an email-sender (currently reset tokens are minted, hashed, and never delivered to anyone).

**Source:**
- [Section #20 - /register Self-Admin + Broken ForgotPassword/ResetPassword + RevokedReasonIsReuse Not Persisted](https://github.com/lstbob/tsqr-domain/blob/main/thegoodandthebad-v2.md#20-register-self-admin--broken-forgotpasswordresetpassword--revokedreasonisreuse-not-persisted)

## Task
Two Critical issues: concurrent refresh-token rotation has no optimistic lock, allowing the family chain to fork and defeating reuse detection; `Revoke` on an already-revoked token overwrites `RevokedAt`/`RevokedByIp`, destroying the audit trail.

## Details
**(a)** `RefreshHandler.cs:17-61`, `RefreshTokenRepository.cs:42-57`:
```csharp
var existing = await refreshRepo.GetByTokenHashAsync(tokenHash, ct);
if (existing.RevokedAt is not null) { ... }
if (!existing.IsActive) { ... }
// No lock between the check and Rotate below
var replacement = existing.Rotate(...);
await refreshRepo.AddAsync(replacement, ct);
refreshRepo.Update(existing);   // UPDATE RefreshTokens SET ReplacedByTokenHash=... WHERE Id=@Id
await uow.SaveChangesAsync(ct);
```
The UPDATE has no `xmin` / `RowVersion` / `ReplacedByTokenHash IS NULL` predicate; `ReplacedByTokenHash` is not declared `UNIQUE`. Two concurrent refresh calls with the same parent both pass the `IsActive` check, both `Rotate`, both insert a different replacement, both update the parent — last write wins, leaving one replacement orphaned and the family chain forked. From then on, reuse detection walks a single lineage and silently ignores the orphan branch.

**(b)** `RefreshToken.cs:65-70`, `RefreshHandler.cs:30-35` — `Revoke` always overwrites `RevokedAt`/`RevokedByIp`:
```csharp
public void Revoke(string? byIp = null, bool reuseDetected = false) {
    RevokedAt = DateTime.UtcNow;          // always overwrites
    RevokedByIp = byIp ?? "unknown";      // always overwrites
}
// RefreshHandler reuse branch:
if (existing.RevokedAt is not null) {
    existing.Revoke(cmd.ClientIp, reuseDetected: true);  // re-revokes an already-revoked token
}
```
When reuse is detected, the original (already-rotated, already-revoked) token's `RevokedAt` (legitimate rotation time) and `RevokedByIp` (legitimate client IP) are overwritten with the attacker's IP and the current timestamp. The exact data needed for incident forensics is destroyed.

## Affected Files
- `src/TSQR.Autheo.Infrastructure/Dapper/Repositories/RefreshTokenRepository.cs:42-57`
- `src/TSQR.Autheo.Domain/Aggregates/RefreshTokenAggregate/RefreshToken.cs:65-70`
- `src/TSQR.Autheo.Application/Commands/RefreshHandler.cs:17-61`
- `infra/init.sql` (declare `UNIQUE` on `RefreshTokens.ReplacedByTokenHash`)

## Suggested Approach
1. Add `WHERE Id = @Id AND ReplacedByTokenHash IS NULL` to the parent-token UPDATE; capture affected rows; on 0-affected, treat as concurrent rotation → trigger reuse detection and revoke the family.
2. Guard `RefreshToken.Revoke`: if `RevokedAt is not null`, only update `RevokedReasonIsReuse = true` (do NOT overwrite `RevokedAt`/`RevokedByIp`).
3. Add `UNIQUE` on `RefreshTokens.ReplacedByTokenHash` (partial, where not null).
4. Add a unit test simulating concurrent refresh calls and verifying the family chain cannot fork.

**Source:**
- [Section #21 - Concurrent Refresh Token Rotation Has No Optimistic Lock + Revoke Overwrites Audit](https://github.com/lstbob/tsqr-domain/blob/main/thegoodandthebad-v2.md#21-concurrent-refresh-token-rotation-has-no-optimistic-lock--revoke-overwrites-audit)
